WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.860 --> 00:00:13.023
Hey, Little Fox readers!

2
00:00:13.863 --> 00:00:14.403
I'm Jake.

3
00:00:15.103 --> 00:00:16.044
Yesterday in Mr.

4
00:00:16.104 --> 00:00:21.325
Fay's class we learned about the Louvre, one of the biggest art museums in the whole world.

5
00:00:22.185 --> 00:00:26.507
I love to draw, so I was pretty excited when I heard we'd be learning about an art

6
00:00:26.567 --> 00:00:27.587
museum in France.

7
00:00:28.527 --> 00:00:30.928
And I'm not the only one who gets excited about art.

8
00:00:31.752 --> 00:00:38.138
Mr. Fay told us that 8.5 million people from all over the world visit the Louvre each year.

9
00:00:39.019 --> 00:00:41.260
Now that's what I call a lot of visitors!

10
00:00:42.662 --> 00:00:46.825
The Louvre is located in the heart of Paris, on the right bank of the Seine River.

11
00:00:47.666 --> 00:00:53.231
It's home to around 35,000 pieces of art and nearly 13 kilometers of galleries.

12
00:00:54.172 --> 00:00:58.876
That means it would take almost a year for a visitor to see everything inside the museum!

13
00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:04.741
When the Louvre was first built, way back in 1190, it was designed to be a fortress to

14
00:01:04.801 --> 00:01:06.502
protect Paris from its enemies.

15
00:01:07.482 --> 00:01:12.103
At the time Paris was the largest city in Europe, and over the next two hundred years, the

16
00:01:12.143 --> 00:01:13.464
city continued to grow.

17
00:01:14.344 --> 00:01:17.525
Eventually the Louvre wasn't big enough to protect the entire city.

18
00:01:18.445 --> 00:01:23.426
So, in 1364, King Charles V decided to turn it into a palace.

19
00:01:24.827 --> 00:01:31.597
During the centuries that followed, members of the French royalty continued changing the Louvre, demolishing some sections and

20
00:01:31.657 --> 00:01:35.700
adding new buildings and wings, along with gardens and fancy decorations.

21
00:01:36.621 --> 00:01:44.206
French monarchs also collected artwork, filling the Louvre with colorful tapestries, elaborate carvings, and all kinds of incredible

22
00:01:44.286 --> 00:01:51.051
paintings. Eventually the royal court decided to move to a new palace, abandoning the Louvre for a while.

23
00:01:51.951 --> 00:01:58.236
Finally, in 1793, government officials decided to turn the buildings into a museum for the public.

24
00:01:59.572 --> 00:02:01.533
Today the Louvre has three main wings.

25
00:02:02.373 --> 00:02:06.895
They're arranged like a giant horseshoe, and in the courtyard in the middle of the wings is a

26
00:02:06.995 --> 00:02:08.216
big glass pyramid.

27
00:02:09.096 --> 00:02:12.838
Inside the Louvre, visitors can see art from many different time periods.

28
00:02:13.698 --> 00:02:21.201
There's a helmet worn by a real Roman gladiator; Egyptian statues over 4000 years old; and a sword

29
00:02:21.461 --> 00:02:23.802
used at the coronations of French kings.

30
00:02:24.723 --> 00:02:27.944
Mr. Fay also showed us some pictures of mummies at the museum.

31
00:02:28.840 --> 00:02:33.162
Melissa said they looked really creepy, but I thought they looked really cool!

32
00:02:33.943 --> 00:02:39.026
Most people visiting the Louvre stop to see the most famous painting in the world—the Mona Lisa.

33
00:02:39.906 --> 00:02:44.349
It's a small portrait of a woman, painted by Leonardo da Vinci in 1506.

34
00:02:45.329 --> 00:02:50.812
One reason the painting is famous is because da Vinci's brushwork makes the background look very realistic.

35
00:02:52.093 --> 00:02:57.836
But the main reason for the Mona Lisa's popularity is probably the mysterious smile on the woman's face.

36
00:02:58.812 --> 00:03:01.534
A lot of people think she's smiling because she has a secret.

37
00:03:02.255 --> 00:03:03.936
Hmm What do you think?

38
00:03:04.917 --> 00:03:12.663
On August 21, 1911, a former worker at the Louvre, Vincenzo Peruggia, actually managed to steal the Mona

39
00:03:12.703 --> 00:03:16.927
Lisa. Peruggia went to the museum one day, pretending to be a visitor.

40
00:03:17.667 --> 00:03:21.410
When the museum closed for the night, he hid and slept there overnight.

41
00:03:22.191 --> 00:03:27.255
The next morning he simply removed the painting from the wall, concealed it under his clothing, and walked

42
00:03:27.335 --> 00:03:27.936
out the door!

43
00:03:28.852 --> 00:03:32.574
I guess they didn't have any high-tech security cameras or alarms back then.

44
00:03:33.895 --> 00:03:38.858
The Mona Lisa wasn't found until two years later, when Peruggia made the mistake of trying to sell

45
00:03:38.898 --> 00:03:41.680
it to an antiques dealer who reported him to the police.

46
00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:46.723
Ever since then the Mona Lisa has been kept behind some very strong glass.

47
00:03:47.623 --> 00:03:52.906
But it still attracts huge crowds, with thousands of visitors trying to catch a glimpse of the mysterious

48
00:03:52.966 --> 00:03:53.927
woman every day.

49
00:03:54.827 --> 00:03:59.771
The Raft of the Medusa by Théodore Géricault is another well-known painting at the Louvre.

50
00:04:00.611 --> 00:04:05.975
But this isn't one you could sneak out of a museum—it's almost five meters high and over seven

51
00:04:06.035 --> 00:04:06.956
meters wide!

52
00:04:08.316 --> 00:04:13.980
I found out that in 1939, the painting's enormous size caused an enormous problem.

53
00:04:14.821 --> 00:04:19.984
World War II had begun, and museum workers were worried about the valuable artwork inside the Louvre.

54
00:04:20.825 --> 00:04:24.868
So they decided to pack it up and move it to castles around France for safekeeping.

55
00:04:25.696 --> 00:04:28.837
Workers placed The Raft of the Medusa on the back of an open truck.

56
00:04:29.658 --> 00:04:34.040
On the drive out to the countryside, the tall painting knocked down some electrical wires.

57
00:04:34.860 --> 00:04:37.942
This caused the electricity to go out in an entire city!

58
00:04:39.322 --> 00:04:44.304
Another popular item at the Louvre is the Venus de Milo, a marble statue that was discovered by

59
00:04:44.404 --> 00:04:50.607
accident. In 1820 a farmer on the island of Melos was digging in the ground when he uncovered

60
00:04:50.647 --> 00:04:52.068
a statue in two pieces.

61
00:04:53.012 --> 00:04:57.835
The whole statue was carefully dug out, and then presented to King Louis XVIII of France.

62
00:04:58.776 --> 00:05:00.597
The king later donated it to the Louvre.

63
00:05:01.478 --> 00:05:06.081
Experts believe that the Venus de Milo was carved sometime around 100 BC.

64
00:05:06.902 --> 00:05:10.984
The statue is most likely of Aphrodite, the Greek goddess of love and beauty.

65
00:05:11.805 --> 00:05:16.909
But nobody knows this for sure because the statue is missing some parts, including its arms.

66
00:05:18.269 --> 00:05:21.712
Last night I went to the library and brought home a book about the Louvre.

67
00:05:22.504 --> 00:05:26.487
It has pictures of some of the artwork at the museum, and I practiced drawing a bunch of

68
00:05:26.548 --> 00:05:29.810
things. Oh, and I also created my own drawing.

69
00:05:30.551 --> 00:05:36.055
Check it out: it's a picture of Vincenzo Peruggia stealing the Mona Lisa while being chased by an

70
00:05:36.115 --> 00:05:37.036
Egyptian mummy!

71
00:05:37.937 --> 00:05:39.959
I hope you enjoyed learning more about the Louvre.

72
00:05:40.799 --> 00:05:43.381
See you again soon for another landmarks lesson!
